{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A","reason":"A lies on the circle, so OA is a radius. Proving OA perpendicular to l establishes that l is tangent at A.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_261_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_064"],"id":"m1","label":"Apply the tangency criterion","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_261"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","cnm_250","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A","reason":"If OH is perpendicular to l and OH equals the radius, then H lies on the circle and l is tangent at H. Perpendicularity alone in B does not establish that the foot lies on the circle.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_261_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_065"],"id":"m1","label":"Draw a perpendicular and prove its length","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_261","cnm_260"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","cnm_260","cnm_065"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"1","reason":"The stated perpendicularity makes l tangent at A, so A is its only common point with the circle.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_261_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_064"],"id":"m1","label":"Recognize a tangent","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_261","cnm_260"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","cnm_260","cnm_250","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (tangent)","reason":"5² + 12² = 13², so OA is perpendicular to AB. Since A is on the circle, line AB is tangent at A.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_261_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_064"],"id":"m1","label":"Establish perpendicularity from the lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_177","cnm_261"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_261","cnm_250","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: AP²=PB·AB","reason":"Let r=OB and d=AD=AP. Then AB=2r, AO=d+r, and (d+r)²=(2r)²+r². Hence d²=4r²-2rd=AB(AB-AP)=AB·PB, proving A.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"tangent-secant-product","label":"Use tangent-secant power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-secant-product":["cnm_261","math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","math:power-of-a-point"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 10/3","reason":"AB=√(12²+5²)=13. If T is the tangency point on AB, right triangles AOT and ABC are similar. Hence r/(12-r)=5/13, so 13r=60-5r and r=10/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_8_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-tangent-lengths","label":"Use equal tangents and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-tangent-lengths":["cnm_261","cnm_263"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","cnm_263"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. For every 0°<θ<60°","reason":"Put r=AB=AD. The sine rule gives AC=2r cosθ and BC=r(4cos²θ-1). With C at coordinate 0 on line CB, the two circle intersections have coordinate sum 2AC cosθ=4r cos²θ. Since B has coordinate BC, E has coordinate r, so CE=r=AD. At θ=45°, E=B and the same equality holds.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_062"],"id":"construct-two-isosceles-triangles","label":"Construct two isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"construct-two-isosceles-triangles":["cnm_138","cnm_115","cnm_261"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_138","cnm_115","cnm_261","cnm_250","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, DF need not bisect ∠CDA","reason":"Write ∠A=α. The diameter gives ∠CDA=90°. Tangent geometry yields FC=FD=FA, ∠FDA=α, ∠CFD=2α, and ∠BCD=α. Thus F is the midpoint of CA and the other stated relations hold, but DF bisects ∠CDA only when α=45°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1965_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"equal-tangents-angle-chase","label":"Equal tangents and angle chasing","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-tangents-angle-chase":["cnm_261","cnm_263","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_117"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","cnm_263","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_117"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"hypotenuse-midpoint-construction","label":"Construct the hypotenuse midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hypotenuse-midpoint-construction":["cnm_187","cnm_137","cnm_261","cnm_117"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_187","cnm_137","cnm_261","cnm_117"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 4.8","reason":"Since 6² + 8² = 10², ∠C = 90° and the altitude to AB is h = 6·8/10 = 4.8. For tangency point T, h ≤ CT ≤ 2r. Equality is attained by the circle whose diameter joins C to its perpendicular foot on AB. Finally, ∠QCR = 90°, so QR is a diameter and equals h = 4.8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_064","cnm_260"],"id":"altitude-diameter-bound","label":"Bound the diameter and construct the smallest circle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-diameter-bound":["cnm_065","cnm_250","cnm_261","cnm_254"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_065","cnm_250","cnm_261","cnm_254","cnm_177","cnm_p148","cnm_064","cnm_260"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 5/2","reason":"Let T be the tangency point and AE = x. Equal tangents give CT = CB = 2 and ET = EA = x, so CE = 2+x. In right triangle CDE, (2+x)² = 2²+(2-x)². This gives x = 1/2 and CE = 5/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_250","cnm_262","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:equal-tangent-lengths:aff6b6b05c12","label":"Equal tangent lengths","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-tangent-lengths":["cnm_261","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_042","cnm_191","cnm_250","cnm_262","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 1679","reason":"Let O be the midpoint of AB and T the contact point on AD. The horizontal offset of AD is (92-19)/2=73/2, so AT=46·(73/2)/x=1679/x. The condition AT≤x gives x²≥1679, with equality when T=D.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1992_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_132","cnm_260"],"id":"solution:similar-right-triangles:c1a78cba5293","label":"Locate the tangency points","methodDirectNodeIds":{"similar-right-triangles":["cnm_262","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","cnm_261"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_262","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_174","cnm_261","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_132","cnm_260"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 5/2","reason":"Let P be the tangency point and x = AE. Equal tangents give CP = CB = 2 and EP = EA = x, so CE = 2 + x. In right triangle CDE, (2 + x)^2 = 4 + (2 - x)^2. Hence x = 1/2 and CE = 5/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_250","cnm_260","cnm_148"],"id":"solution:equal-tangents-and-pythagoras:caeecf6fc330","label":"Equal tangents and a right triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-tangents-and-pythagoras":["cnm_261","cnm_262","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_261","cnm_262","cnm_263","cnm_174","cnm_042","cnm_191","cnm_250","cnm_260","cnm_148"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":11,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 51","reason":"The acute conditions are 4<x+y<14 and (x-4.5)^2+(y-4.5)^2>12.5. The first-quadrant band has area (14²-4²)/2=90. The diameter-AB disk lies entirely in that band and the first quadrant, so the area is 90-12.5π≈50.73, whose closest integer is 51.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p101","cnm_115","cnm_p148","cnm_p192","cnm_261"],"id":"solution:geometric-acute-locus:cce6deb7c4d1","label":"Find the region using perpendicular lines and a diameter circle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"geometric-acute-locus":["cnm_084","cnm_p150","cnm_h168","cnm_h169","cnm_h173","cnm_254","math:linear-half-plane-systems"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_084","cnm_p150","cnm_h168","cnm_h169","cnm_h173","cnm_254","math:linear-half-plane-systems","cnm_p101","cnm_115","cnm_p148","cnm_p192","cnm_261"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_261","status":"mapped","total":13}
